.TH PROCESSGROUP 1 "November 2026" "Custom Shell" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
processgroup \- launch, monitor and control a batch of processes as one unit
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B processgroup start
.RB [ \-q ]
.RB [ \-d ]
.RB [ \-n
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\- ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARG ...]
.br
.B processgroup start
.RB [ \-q ]
.RB [ \-d ]
.B \-f
.I FILE
.br
.B processgroup status
.I PGID
.br
.B processgroup monitor
.RB [ \-i
.IR SECS ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR COUNT ]
.I PGID
.br
.B processgroup signal
.I PGID SIGNAL
.br
.B processgroup terminate
.RB [ \-w
.IR SECS ]
.I PGID
.br
.B processgroup help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B processgroup
manages a batch of related processes as a single unit.
It places every process of the batch in one new process group, so the whole
batch can be inspected, signalled or shut down with one command instead of
one command per process.
.PP
The process group ID (PGID) is the handle for a batch.
.B start
prints it, and every other subcommand takes it as an argument.
No state file is kept: members are found by scanning
.IR /proc ,
so the commands work from any shell or terminal, and even after the program
that started the batch has exited.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B start
Launch processes in a new process group and print the group ID.
The first process becomes the group leader.
All commands are checked (searched for in
.BR PATH )
before anything is started; if one cannot be found or executed the whole
batch is refused and nothing is launched.
Each process starts with default signal handling and with standard input
connected to
.IR /dev/null .
The processes keep running after
.B processgroup
exits.
.TP
.B status
Print one table of every process in the group:
process ID, parent ID, state (running, sleeping, stopped, zombie, ...),
CPU time in seconds, resident memory in kilobytes, thread count and the
command line, followed by totals.
.TP
.B monitor
Print the same table repeatedly and add a
.B %CPU
column computed from the CPU time used since the previous sample.
Monitoring ends when the group has no processes left, when
.I COUNT
samples have been shown, or when the user presses Ctrl+C.
Interrupting
.B monitor
never affects the monitored group.
.TP
.B signal
Send one signal to every process in the group with a single
.BR killpg (2)
call.
.TP
.B terminate
Shut the group down politely, then forcefully.
.B SIGTERM
is sent to the group, followed by
.B SIGCONT
so that stopped processes wake up and can act on it.
Every 100 ms the group is checked; when no live process remains the command
succeeds.
If processes are still alive after the wait time,
.B SIGKILL
is sent to the group.
The result reports how many processes exited on
.B SIGTERM
and how many needed
.BR SIGKILL .
.TP
.B help
Print a command summary.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS start
.TP
.BI \-n " N"
Run
.I N
copies of
.I COMMAND
(1 to 1000, default 1).
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-f .
.TP
.BI \-f " FILE"
Run every command listed in
.IR FILE ,
one process per line (see
.BR "BATCH FILE FORMAT" ).
Cannot be combined with
.B \-n
or a command on the command line.
.TP
.B \-q
Quiet: print only the group ID, so that it can be captured in a script.
.TP
.B \-d
Discard the standard output and standard error of the started processes.
Without it the processes keep the caller's output open, which makes a shell
construct such as
.B PGID=$(processgroup start \-q ...)
wait until all of them have finished.
Use
.B \-qd
together when capturing the group ID.
.TP
.B \-h
Show help for
.BR start .
.PP
Use
.B \-\-
to end option parsing when
.I COMMAND
itself begins with a dash.
.SS monitor
.TP
.BI \-i " SECS"
Seconds between samples, from 0.1 to 3600 (default 1).
.TP
.BI \-c " COUNT"
Stop after
.I COUNT
samples (default: run until the group ends or Ctrl+C).
.SS terminate
.TP
.BI \-w " SECS"
Seconds to wait after
.B SIGTERM
before sending
.B SIGKILL
(0 to 3600, default 5).
.PP
Each subcommand also accepts
.BR \-h .
.SH SIGNALS
The
.I SIGNAL
argument of
.B signal
may be written with or without the
.B SIG
prefix and in any letter case, or as a number from 0 to 64.
Recognised names:
.BR HUP ,
.BR INT ,
.BR QUIT ,
.BR KILL ,
.BR TERM ,
.BR STOP ,
.BR CONT ,
.BR TSTP ,
.BR USR1 ,
.BR USR2
and
.BR ALRM .
Signal 0 sends nothing and only tests that the group exists and may be
signalled.
.PP
.B processgroup
itself catches
.B SIGINT
and
.B SIGTERM
while running
.B monitor
and
.BR terminate ,
so that it can stop cleanly instead of being killed in the middle of its work.
.SH BATCH FILE FORMAT
A file given to
.B start \-f
contains one command per line.
Arguments are separated by white space.
Blank lines and lines whose first non-blank character is
.B #
are ignored.
There is no quoting or shell expansion.
Limits: 1000 commands per file, 64 words per command, 1023 characters per
line.
.PP
Example file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
# two sleeps and a compile
sleep 60
sleep 90
gcc \-O2 \-o /tmp/a.out prog.c
.fi
.RE
.SH SAFETY
.B signal
and
.B terminate
refuse to act on group 1 or lower, on the process group of
.B processgroup
itself, and on the process group of the shell that started it.
This prevents the batch tools from taking down the system or the user's
own terminal session.
Signals can only be sent to groups the user is permitted to signal;
.BR EPERM
and
.BR ESRCH
errors are reported with an explanatory message.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A runtime error: command not found, group not found or empty, a refused
target, a failed system call, or processes that could not be terminated.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error: unknown subcommand or option, missing or invalid argument.
.SH EXAMPLES
Start five copies of a long-running command and note the group ID:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ processgroup start \-n 5 \-\- sleep 300
Started 5 process(es) in process group 4821.
.fi
.RE
.PP
Show the group, then watch its CPU use every two seconds, ten times:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ processgroup status 4821
$ processgroup monitor \-i 2 \-c 10 4821
.fi
.RE
.PP
Pause every process at once, then resume them:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ processgroup signal 4821 STOP
$ processgroup signal 4821 CONT
.fi
.RE
.PP
Shut the group down, allowing 3 seconds before
.BR SIGKILL :
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ processgroup terminate \-w 3 4821
Group 4821 terminated: 5 exited on SIGTERM, 0 needed SIGKILL.
.fi
.RE
.PP
Capture the group ID in a script:
.PP
.RS
.nf
GID=$(processgroup start \-qd \-n 3 \-\- ./worker)
processgroup monitor \-c 5 "$GID"
processgroup terminate "$GID"
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES FOR THE CUSTOM SHELL
The command is implemented by the function
.B int processgroup_main(int argc, char *argv[])
in
.IR processgroup.c .
The shell calls it with
.I argv[0]
set to the command name,
.I argv[1]
the subcommand, and
.I argv
terminated by a null pointer.
Compile with
.B \-DPROCESSGROUP_NO_MAIN
to leave out the stand-alone
.BR main ()
when linking into the shell.
Option parsing with
.BR getopt (3)
resets
.B optind
before every parse, so the shell's own use of
.BR getopt (3)
is not disturbed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /proc/<pid>/stat
State, parent, process group, CPU time, thread count and resident pages.
.TP
.I /proc/<pid>/cmdline
Command line shown in the tables.
.SH BUGS
Resource monitoring requires a Linux
.BR proc (5)
file system.
Process groups can be changed by the processes themselves
.RB ( setpgid (2));
a process that moves to another group is no longer part of the batch.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR setpgid (2),
.BR killpg (2),
.BR kill (2),
.BR sigaction (2),
.BR waitpid (2),
.BR getopt (3),
.BR proc (5),
.BR signal (7)
